Z||000005||Pilrulz||11-01-2001||05:25 PM||||my car did the same thing, do i really have to replace the motor?||||reg||1
Z||000006||bocefas||11-01-2001||06:03 PM||||Yes, you do...replace or rebuild...if the contacts were dirty the motor would work erraticly or not at all.  The plastic gears/bushings(depending on the year) are stripped and not allowing the motor to shut off.||||reg||1
Z||000007||NEO Cesek Racing||11-01-2001||08:56 PM||||hi all, I've new to the forum. i've only had my Fiero for about 9 months so i'm learning as i go here.<BR>anyway, i had a question for bocefas about the headlight motors. my passenger side motor just went out on me yesterday, i'm about to replace it . you said you recommend upgrading to a newer style motor, what style do you mean. all i can find anywhere are AC Delco motors.  forgive my lack of knowledge i'm new at this, but any help would be appreciated. thanks||||reg||10
Z||000008||Borgio||11-01-2001||09:42 PM||||What year do you have? The motors were redone in '87. But just because one isn't working doesn't mean you have to replace it. Have you fiddled with it at all? Sometimes they get stuck. The pre '87 motors have a hole in the top black piece. If you need to get the light up temporarily you can just wind them up and down with a pen. But sometimes when you start to do that it will just start working. Mine are '87s and I've still had problems but just messing with them usually fixes it and now they work fine. I'm not sure, but I think firebirds of that era used the same motors, so you can easily find a replacement. Someone correct me if I'm wrong about any of this stuff.   <P>------------------<BR>Jake||||reg||1
Z||000009||NEO Cesek Racing||11-01-2001||09:50 PM||||i have an 84 SE. i know the knob you're talking about but the problem is not that it's sticking, it's that it won't QUIT spinning unless i disconnect it. i think somone said something about brushings being worn down? a fiero buddy of mine told me to go in there and spin the brushing around so it uses the un-worn side and it'll work fine. any suggestions?<P>------------------<BR>I used to have a handle <BR>on life but it broke.||||reg||1
Z||000010||DR650SE||11-01-2001||10:14 PM||||Crap.  I hope I don't have to replace the motor.  I was bleeding the clutch on my other 86 se and the slave cylinder had a small crack in it(Explains why I could never bleed it enough) and it broke at the valve.  Then a spark plug wire broke when I was taking it out so I need to get a set of those and a set of plugs.  I'm ganna try and get the light motor working and If I do I will let you all know what I do and such.  Thanks.<P>------------------<BR>2 Black 86 SE's V-6 4-SPD||||reg||1
Z||000011||CountFiero||11-02-2001||07:45 PM|||| <BLOCKQUOTE><font size="1" face="Verdana, Arial, Helvetica">quote:</font><HR>Originally posted by DR650SE:<BR><B>Whats up with that?  When the head light goes down the motor won't quit spinning.  Right now its outside with the fuseout.  When I drove it home i turned the lights on and off a few min. later.  Now the motor runs constantly and so I drive with them up and turn them off and take the fuse out when i park.  Please help.  Thanks.<P>               Kindest Regards,<BR>                  Dan Tillman<P></B><HR></BLOCKQUOTE><BR>This is a common problem.  I just installed a "bypass" switch under the dash.  Cost about $5.00.  The problem is caused by a gear inside the motor assembly that loses a tooth or two.  One guy suggested opening the motor housing and lifting out the gear and rotating it so that the missing part of the gear is "un used" when the motor is activated.  This avoids having to buy a new motor.  The gear in question supposedly only rotates for PART of 1 entire revolution so it is possible to use the "good" part of the gear and "avoid" the part that is missing teeth.  A good option IF it is possible.  OR just do like I did and install a bypass switch to the motor.  You have to switch OFF the running motor when you close (turn off) the light but it works and is CHEAP.<P>||||reg||7
Z||000012||blazin'||11-02-2001||10:56 PM||||Am I wrong in saying that you just need to replace the small sensors on the motor?? I just completed the same project today! I just switched the sensors and they work fine! Am I wrong or just really confused! Lol||||reg||1
Z||000013||72vinman||11-02-2001||11:23 PM||||My passenger headlight did the same thing. It would just continue to spin. Order the rebuild kit from <A HREF="http://www.rodneydickman.com" TARGET=_blank>www.rodneydickman.com</A> <BR>Trust me you will be glad you did. It is inexpensive and they will work better than new. Rodney is super fast with his shipping too. You will be very pleased with the results. No need to buy new motors or redo the electrical system. ||||reg||14
Z||000014||SLYGUY||11-03-2001||01:03 PM|||| <BLOCKQUOTE><font size="1" face="Verdana, Arial, Helvetica">quote:</font><HR>Originally posted by bocefas:<BR><B>The plastic gears/bushings(depending on the year) are stripped and not allowing the motor to shut off.</B><HR></BLOCKQUOTE><P>My left headlight would dance around before making it to the fully deployed position. When it stopped working completely I ordered the bushing kit from Rodney and it worked like a charm. <BR>Order them.<P>Sly<P>||||reg||1
